# Every classification this tree makes, against the set of classes it declares, over every loaded
# document. One row per (relation, class): the relation that classifies, the type its classes
# belong to, what one counted row IS, and how many landed in the class.
#
# The classes are part of the function
#   A classification is a function from rows to classes. The partition laws in algebra/ prove that
#   each row landed in exactly one class, which survives any renaming of the classes. Which classes
#   there are is the type's job: each set here is an enum, so a misspelt class fails when the query
#   is parsed. Neither can show which classes are empty, and an empty class is often the finding.
#
# Pooled on purpose, and that is what makes it the contract's population
#   This counts the corpus and the fixtures together, because the question it answers is whether
#   anything in this repository computes the class at all. A class no document reaches under any
#   scope is a class no relation here can produce, and folds/class_subjects.sqlc holds
#   epistemics/class_domain.sqlc to exactly that.
#   ⛔ It is therefore NOT a reading of the evidence. What a chosen set of documents shows is a
#   different question with a different answer, and reports/class_census.sqlc is where it is put;
#   `assets/fixtures/README.md` argues why a dark state means opposite things in the two places.
#
# `subject` is the unit, and a count without one is not a number
#   The eight relations count in six units, so `balls` is comparable down a relation's own arm and
#   nowhere else. epistemics/class_sets.sqlc declares the unit and this carries it beside the
#   count so a reader never has to infer it. The join is LEFT so a relation missing from that
#   roster arrives with a NULL subject and is reported by algebra/class_domain.sqlc, rather than
#   having its classes vanish.
#
# `filed_balls` is how the `filed` flag is held
#   epistemics/class_sets.sqlc declares whether a relation classifies rows a document filed, and
#   this counts the balls that actually carry one. The two are compared by
#   algebra/class_domain.sqlc, so a relation whose arm stops carrying a filing, or starts, is a
#   failure rather than a report that quietly narrows. It is the same aggregate, one more column,
#   which is why the flag costs no extra pass over the eight arms.
#
# `ord` is the declared order, and it is a column because it cannot be recovered
#   Each type's members come out in the order the type declares them, which is the `CASE`
#   precedence of the relation that assigns them. A consumer grouping alone has nothing to sort
#   on but the class text, which is alphabetical and says nothing, and precedence is the argument
#   `layers/exposure_scope.sqlc` makes in prose, so losing it loses a reading.
#
# Why `codomain` is `pg_typeof`
#   Taking the type from the class itself lets a caller hold every enum in schema `public` against
#   this relation using the catalog, which Postgres writes. A query-side class set added to
#   schema.ddl without an arm in epistemics/class_cells.sqlc fails `examples/combinatorics/main.rs`.
-- epistemics/class_cells.sqlc counted per class over every loaded document, with its declared unit.
SELECT k.relation, k.codomain, k.class, s.subject,
       count(k.ball)   AS balls,
       count(k.filing) AS filed_balls,
       k.ord
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/class_cells.sqlc)
) k
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/class_sets.sqlc)
) s ON s.relation = k.relation
GROUP BY k.relation, k.codomain, k.class, s.subject, k.ord
